1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
To think, to reason, makes us human, sets us apart from the rest of nature.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
People communicate in intricate ways about complex subjects,

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and at the center of it all, a thinking machine, the computer.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
With it, our thoughts can be recorded.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
What once seemed impossible, now seems commonplace.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,400
Today, computers are capable of amazing tasks that boggle the mind.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:37,600
But the evolution of a thinking machine, a computer, engaged some of the greatest minds of the last two centuries.

8
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:48,560
The story of the computer contains squandered chances, fortunate accidents, frequent missteps, fantastic insights, and unprecedented genius.

9
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:52,700
It is the story of a machine that helps us win a war.

10
00:00:52,701 --> 00:00:56,940
figures in the creation of weapons of inconceivable destruction

11
00:00:56,941 --> 00:00:59,100
and sends us into space.

12
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,400
The birth of the computer spawned the growth of whole industries,

13
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:06,120
led to the world's greatest personal fortune,

14
00:01:06,121 --> 00:01:10,000
and will increasingly affect the lives of everyone on Earth.

15
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:17,580
Thinking Machines, the creation of the computer.

16
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:49,440
Our world is increasingly filled with countless wonders that would not have been possible without one machine, the computer.

17
00:01:49,441 --> 00:01:59,780
Although computers are enormously intricate, their most basic components consist of simple devices that can be switched to either one of two states, on or off.

18
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:07,820
The computer creates its magic by calculating with a speed and accuracy that far surpasses its human inventors.

19
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:16,940
Computers stagger the mind with their complexity, but simply put, a computer takes information, processes it, and then outputs a result.

20
00:02:16,941 --> 00:02:21,940
It's all done with a unique partnership of hardware and software.

21
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:23,505
Hardware comes in boxes.

22
00:02:23,506 --> 00:02:27,940
They're the physical components, such as the monitor and hard drive.

23
00:02:27,941 --> 00:02:29,766
Software comes on disks.

24
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:34,940
Software consists of instructions that tell the computer what to do.

25
00:02:34,941 --> 00:02:40,940
One way to start this computational partnership is to type on the keyboard, providing input.

26
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:45,940
The input is picked up by the central processing unit, or CPU, the computer's brain.

27
00:02:45,941 --> 00:02:52,120
brain. Using instructions provided by software, the CPU processes the input.

28
00:02:52,121 --> 00:02:57,880
The magic behind the CPU is its blinding speed. Modern processors are measured in

29
00:02:57,881 --> 00:03:03,720
MIPS, millions of instructions per second. While processing, the CPU may

30
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:09,100
retrieve data stored in random access memory known as RAM or data stored on a

31
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:13,780
hard drive. Modern RAM is so quick that every second it can send the equivalent

32
00:03:13,781 --> 00:03:18,700
of 10,000 typewritten pages of information to the CPU.

33
00:03:18,701 --> 00:03:22,420
And modern hard drives can store the equivalent of 250,000

34
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:25,160
pages of typewritten material.

35
00:03:25,161 --> 00:03:28,360
After processing, the CPU outputs information,

36
00:03:28,361 --> 00:03:30,860
often on a monitor.

37
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:33,020
The whole procedure is usually so quick

38
00:03:33,021 --> 00:03:36,060
that it appears instantaneous.

39
00:03:36,061 --> 00:03:38,180
Today, computers are so commonplace,

40
00:03:38,181 --> 00:03:39,760
we take them for granted.

41
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:42,300
But not long ago, computers only existed

42
00:03:42,301 --> 00:03:46,680
in the imagination of a few visionaries.

43
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:51,060
The search for machines that could figure quickly and accurately has seized the human

44
00:03:51,061 --> 00:03:53,760
imagination for thousands of years.

45
00:03:53,761 --> 00:03:58,860
In fact, the computer's family tree has roots so deep in the past, it's impossible to know

46
00:03:58,861 --> 00:04:08,840
exactly where they begin.

47
00:04:08,841 --> 00:04:15,100
By the early 19th century, the European Industrial Revolution was well underway.

48
00:04:15,101 --> 00:04:20,500
And the development in production and commerce came from the maturing fields of engineering,

49
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:24,920
navigation, surveying, finance and science.

50
00:04:24,921 --> 00:04:30,620
The practical application of these fields relied on volume after volume of tables.

51
00:04:30,621 --> 00:04:36,360
Tables for trigonometry, tides, interest rates, multiplication and gravity.

52
00:04:36,361 --> 00:04:37,760
Tables were critical.

53
00:04:37,761 --> 00:04:42,500
The actual figuring was done by people who specialized in mathematical computation.

54
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:46,040
Surprisingly, these people had a familiar job title.

55
00:04:46,041 --> 00:04:48,440
They were called computers.

56
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:55,040
These human computers toiled over their tables incessantly, monotonously, and made mistakes.

57
00:04:55,041 --> 00:04:58,460
Typically tables were full of errors.

58
00:04:58,461 --> 00:05:03,600
The requirement for accurate tables introduced one of the most eccentric and brilliant figures

59
00:05:03,601 --> 00:05:07,240
into the story of computers, Charles Babbage.

60
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:11,020
Oh, Babbage was an extraordinary scientist.

61
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:12,400
I mean, Babbage was a great scientist.

62
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:15,220
Babbage was 100 years ahead of his time.

63
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:17,100
You can't say that about many people,

64
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:18,600
but you could say that about Babbage.

65
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:22,700
Charles Babbage represented that extraordinary element

66
00:05:22,701 --> 00:05:25,580
of British society, the scientist-aristocrat.

67
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,700
Many were known for their eccentricities,

68
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:30,440
and Babbage was no exception.

69
00:05:30,441 --> 00:05:36,920
As a youth, Babbage devised footwear of hinged boards intended to allow him to walk on water.

70
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:42,460
Never one to shirk adventure, he tried them out himself, but flipped over and nearly drowned.

71
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,000
Babbage demonstrated his brilliance in mathematics while attending Trinity College in Cambridge.

72
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:56,660
In 1820, Babbage was checking the accuracy of calculations made for the Royal Astronomical Society and kept finding errors.

73
00:05:57,540 --> 00:06:03,360
He reasoned that a machine could be constructed that would calculate the tables and directly print the results.

74
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:06,980
He called the machine the difference engine.

75
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:13,020
He drew up plans for a section of the device and had it built with his own funds in 1822.

76
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:17,220
Babbage couldn't pay for the construction of the entire device,

77
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:21,000
but since the greatest beneficiaries would be the British government and people,

78
00:06:21,001 --> 00:06:24,820
he made the extraordinary step of petitioning the government for a grant.

79
00:06:24,821 --> 00:06:30,820
In 1823, the Treasury provided the project with start-up funds.

80
00:06:30,821 --> 00:06:34,820
Government support for the computer industry is nothing new.

81
00:06:34,821 --> 00:06:39,820
It's very much a big topic in the news today, and it will continue to be.

82
00:06:39,821 --> 00:06:46,820
Computing is an expensive proposition, and it usually requires some government support if it's going to get anywhere.

83
00:06:46,821 --> 00:06:53,820
Babbage hired a mechanical engineer set to work on a complete design for the Difference engine

84
00:06:53,821 --> 00:06:56,060
and immediately ran into difficulties.

85
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:00,320
The mechanical machine shops of the time were not advanced enough

86
00:07:00,321 --> 00:07:04,300
to produce parts in the precise measurements that Babbage's plans required.

87
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:07,440
So Babbage designed better machine tools,

88
00:07:07,441 --> 00:07:11,300
which would eventually improve the entire state of British tool manufacturing.

89
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:17,380
By 1829, Babbage had spent the 1,500-pound grant from the government

90
00:07:17,381 --> 00:07:19,900
and even more than that from his own funds.

91
00:07:19,901 --> 00:07:24,160
but only a few bits and pieces of machine had been completed.

92
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:28,580
Babbage's project began to attract critics.

93
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:31,860
He was plagued by several problems,

94
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:34,580
one of his problems being his perfectionism,

95
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:39,680
another problem being that his work was not understood

96
00:07:39,681 --> 00:07:43,060
or appreciated by the people of his time.

97
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:46,000
Babbage had many enemies.

98
00:07:46,001 --> 00:07:48,700
Even London's organ grinders despised him

99
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:50,500
because he had tried to have them banned

100
00:07:50,501 --> 00:07:54,340
as their music interfered with his thinking.

101
00:07:54,341 --> 00:07:56,440
But the inventor continued to toil,

102
00:07:56,441 --> 00:07:59,620
and finally, in 1832, there were enough parts

103
00:07:59,621 --> 00:08:01,940
to assemble a section of the engine.

104
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:04,820
It functioned perfectly, solving equations

105
00:08:04,821 --> 00:08:07,720
and producing six-digit results.

106
00:08:07,721 --> 00:08:12,060
But it was only a small part of the proposed machine.

107
00:08:12,061 --> 00:08:14,720
The skyrocketing costs and lack of results

108
00:08:14,721 --> 00:08:19,820
finally made the government pull its support from the project.

109
00:08:19,821 --> 00:08:22,320
Although disappointed by the cancellation,

110
00:08:22,321 --> 00:08:25,240
Babbage had contributed to the project's demise

111
00:08:25,241 --> 00:08:28,040
by suggesting that a new device he had conceived,

112
00:08:28,041 --> 00:08:31,120
the analytical engine, would be vastly superior

113
00:08:31,121 --> 00:08:34,320
to his old design.

114
00:08:34,321 --> 00:08:37,700
Babbage, in hindsight, probably should

115
00:08:37,701 --> 00:08:39,440
have finished the difference engine

116
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:43,280
and seen how far he could have gone with that

117
00:08:43,281 --> 00:08:45,780
before starting the analytical engine.

118
00:08:45,781 --> 00:08:48,820
There's no question that the analytical engine was

119
00:08:48,821 --> 00:08:51,200
more than he could handle.

120
00:08:51,201 --> 00:08:53,780
Babbage was obsessed with his new idea.

121
00:08:53,781 --> 00:08:56,360
With the analytical engine, Babbage asked himself,

122
00:08:56,361 --> 00:08:58,280
why not build a machine that could solve

123
00:08:58,281 --> 00:09:01,200
any mathematical problem?

124
00:09:01,201 --> 00:09:05,080
At the age of 43, Babbage had the vision of a computer,

125
00:09:05,081 --> 00:09:08,700
a vision he pursued for the rest of his life.

126
00:09:08,701 --> 00:09:12,100
The extraordinary fact is that Babbage's overall design

127
00:09:12,101 --> 00:09:18,780
for the analytical engine had many components analogous to those in a modern computer.

128
00:09:18,781 --> 00:09:23,580
The heart of the machine, the mill, made the calculations, like the central processing

129
00:09:23,581 --> 00:09:26,540
unit of modern computers.

130
00:09:26,541 --> 00:09:32,440
An oblong structure, the store, held numbers to be used in the calculations, like modern

131
00:09:32,441 --> 00:09:35,740
computer memory.

132
00:09:35,741 --> 00:09:40,780
Instructions and numbers could be fed into the machine using punch cards.

133
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:44,720
Much of what we know about the workings of the analytical engine came from the writings

134
00:09:44,721 --> 00:09:48,180
of Ada, Countess of Lovelace.

135
00:09:48,181 --> 00:09:55,460
Among the people who understood what Babbage was doing was a woman named Ada Augusta, who

136
00:09:55,461 --> 00:10:00,900
was the daughter of, one of the daughters of Lord Byron, the poet.

137
00:10:00,901 --> 00:10:07,060
She had studied mathematics as a child and had quite a bit of talent.

138
00:10:07,061 --> 00:10:10,760
Ada met Babbage at one of his famous dinner parties that were often attended by the

139
00:10:10,761 --> 00:10:15,520
luminaries of British science and engineering. Babbage demonstrated the

140
00:10:15,521 --> 00:10:19,100
working section of the difference engine for her and she was immediately

141
00:10:19,101 --> 00:10:25,060
captivated by it. She published a simple description of Babbage's vision for the

142
00:10:25,061 --> 00:10:31,700
analytical engine. Ada wrote some descriptions of it and she also

143
00:10:31,701 --> 00:10:36,980
appended to these descriptions a hypothetical way that this machine could

144
00:10:36,981 --> 00:10:38,357
solve an equation.

145
00:10:38,358 --> 00:10:43,940
And on the basis of those descriptions, people often call her the world's

146
00:10:43,941 --> 00:10:48,182
first programmer. But she was never to program a real machine.

147
00:10:48,183 --> 00:10:50,440
As Babbage entered the last years

148
00:10:50,441 --> 00:10:52,994
of his life, his great work was unfinished.

149
00:10:52,995 --> 00:10:56,320
He had become cranky and suffered constant attack by his

150
00:10:56,321 --> 00:11:02,720
many enemies. In 1871, when London's organ grinders discovered that Babbage was ill,

151
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:08,440
they surrounded his house and serenaded him, increasing his agony until he died.

152
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:14,420
Only a small portion of the analytical engine was built in Babbage's lifetime.

153
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:18,880
Babbage's vision of the computer fell into obscurity,

154
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:24,000
and except for the detailed text left by Ada, could well have been forgotten.

155
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:31,180
Babbage's machines, which were never finished, which existed for the most part only on paper,

156
00:11:31,181 --> 00:11:32,760
were proto-computers.

157
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:34,220
They were mechanical.

158
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:35,380
They used gears.

159
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:37,720
They used metal shafts.

160
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:40,360
They weren't computers in our sense of the term.

161
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,280
That is, they weren't electronic digital computers,

162
00:11:42,780 --> 00:11:47,280
but they were abstractly and on paper mechanical computers.

163
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:50,580
It would be nearly 100 years

164
00:11:50,581 --> 00:11:52,940
before a programmable computation device

165
00:11:52,941 --> 00:11:54,380
would again be conceived.

166
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:08,180
In the late 1800s, the idea of a programmable computer was a distant dream.

167
00:12:08,181 --> 00:12:11,180
But the American dream was becoming a reality.

168
00:12:11,181 --> 00:12:15,180
America was on its way to becoming the world's greatest industrial power

169
00:12:15,181 --> 00:12:18,180
and opened its arms to immigrants from other lands.

170
00:12:18,181 --> 00:12:26,180
In the second half of the 19th century, America's population increased 35% each decade.

171
00:12:26,181 --> 00:12:35,180
America's exploding population began to endanger one of its great institutions, the American census.

172
00:12:35,181 --> 00:12:47,180
The census, which is required by the Constitution to be held every ten years, was still being done by old-fashioned people with,

173
00:12:47,181 --> 00:12:50,780
with making check marks on pieces of paper,

174
00:12:50,781 --> 00:12:54,220
and it simply couldn't keep up with the tremendous surge

175
00:12:54,221 --> 00:12:57,700
of population in the U.S.

176
00:12:57,701 --> 00:13:00,700
The crisis reached ahead in 1887.

177
00:13:00,701 --> 00:13:03,700
The Census Bureau was still hand-tallying the data

178
00:13:03,701 --> 00:13:06,300
from the 1880 census.

179
00:13:06,301 --> 00:13:09,500
Desperate for relief, the Bureau pleaded for any method

180
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:13,500
that could speed up the counting of the 1890 census.

181
00:13:13,501 --> 00:13:15,900
The superintendent of the census had proposals

182
00:13:15,901 --> 00:13:19,380
for three systems, so he decided to stage a contest.

183
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:22,920
Two of the systems relied on hand counting.

184
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:26,820
The third, developed by a young, rather humorless,

185
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:29,980
former MIT instructor named Herman Hollerith,

186
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:31,720
used punched cards.

187
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:35,220
Punch cards would one day become the standard method

188
00:13:35,221 --> 00:13:37,840
of feeding high volumes of data into computers.

189
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:42,880
Now, where he got this idea, we are not sure.

190
00:13:42,881 --> 00:13:51,960
He may have been inspired by the fact that a conductor on a railroad punches your ticket when you hand it to him.

191
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:54,580
Hollerith's system beat the others easily.

192
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,880
In the tabulation portion of the test, it was nearly 10 times faster.

193
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:07,480
The Census Bureau leased 56 of Hollerith's machines at $1,000 a year each and put them to work in July 1890.

194
00:14:07,481 --> 00:14:13,480
Census Bureau clerks used Hollerith's machines to punch the cards and then tabulate the results.

195
00:14:13,481 --> 00:14:18,480
Scores of operators were trained to use the puncher quickly and accurately.

196
00:14:18,481 --> 00:14:21,480
The tabulating was done with electricity.

197
00:14:21,481 --> 00:14:26,480
A metal pin that passed through a card hole made electrical contact with a cup of mercury,

198
00:14:26,481 --> 00:14:33,480
completing a circuit that was registered on a tallying device that consisted of rows of clock-like dials.

199
00:14:33,481 --> 00:14:39,220
dials. Hollerith's machines were a step toward the later development of computers.

200
00:14:39,221 --> 00:14:40,480
They significantly

201
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:43,576
sped up the processing of information.

202
00:14:43,577 --> 00:14:46,980
The results of the 1890 census count were a triumph

203
00:14:46,981 --> 00:14:53,280
for Hollerith. In just six weeks, the population count of 62,622,250 was tallied.

204
00:14:53,281 --> 00:14:53,981
Hollerith

205
00:14:53,982 --> 00:14:57,420
150 was tallied.

206
00:14:57,421 --> 00:15:01,920
Hollerith became the talk of the scientific community.

207
00:15:01,921 --> 00:15:05,100
He rented an office and set himself up in business.

208
00:15:05,101 --> 00:15:08,980
He called his new enterprise the Tabulating Machine Company.

209
00:15:08,981 --> 00:15:11,680
Hollerith had the Census Bureau business in his pocket,

210
00:15:11,681 --> 00:15:13,580
and the future looked bright.

211
00:15:13,581 --> 00:15:16,020
But it turned out to be harder than it seemed.

212
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:30,980
Hollerith's natural aptitude for mechanical devices was obvious, but he also proved himself to be a dogged businessman.

213
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:36,980
He drummed up business among one of the biggest industries of the day, the railroads.

214
00:15:36,981 --> 00:15:42,980
With the increase in population and the push west, the railroads had grown into enormous organizations,

215
00:15:42,981 --> 00:15:48,980
with personnel, stations, cars, and customers scattered all across the country.

216
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:55,980
Hundreds of clerks produced tons of paper to help track and manage these vast empires.

217
00:15:55,981 --> 00:16:01,980
Hollerith convinced the New York Central Railroad to try out some of his machines.

218
00:16:01,981 --> 00:16:04,001
The experiment wasn't a success.

219
00:16:04,002 --> 00:16:07,980
Hollerith's machines could compute fast enough for census work,

220
00:16:07,981 --> 00:16:11,980
but couldn't keep up with the speed and volume of the railroad business.

221
00:16:11,981 --> 00:16:14,980
After three months, the machines were removed.

222
00:16:14,981 --> 00:16:17,980
Hollerith was short on capital and faced ruin.

223
00:16:17,981 --> 00:16:21,772
ruin. He moved his family into his mother-in-law's house.

224
00:16:21,773 --> 00:16:24,100
He sold his assets, even his horse,

225
00:16:24,101 --> 00:16:29,780
to raise money to redesign his machines, to improve their speed, reliability, and ability

226
00:16:29,781 --> 00:16:30,919
to make additions.

227
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:35,220
Hollerith even customized the punch cards for business computations,

228
00:16:35,560 --> 00:16:38,972
such as adding columns to store dollars and cents.

229
00:16:38,973 --> 00:16:41,360
After a solid year of tedious work,

230
00:16:41,640 --> 00:16:46,080
Hollerith returned to the New York Central and offered them free use of his new, improved,

231
00:16:46,081 --> 00:16:48,400
and faster computing machines for a year.

232
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:52,600
Within three months, the railroad was convinced

233
00:16:52,601 --> 00:16:54,680
and contracted to lease the machines.

234
00:16:55,880 --> 00:16:58,980
The tabulating machine company was back on track.

235
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:01,860
Hollerith had avoided bankruptcy

236
00:17:01,861 --> 00:17:04,000
and now had more work than he could handle.

237
00:17:05,380 --> 00:17:08,940
And Hollerith, you could say, came along just in time.

238
00:17:08,941 --> 00:17:13,520
It was a combination of his invention making this available,

239
00:17:13,521 --> 00:17:19,160
but also the need out there required something like that.

240
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:21,900
So it was a convergence of the social needs

241
00:17:21,901 --> 00:17:24,320
or the social factors on the one hand

242
00:17:24,321 --> 00:17:27,560
and the inventiveness sort of pushing from the other hand.

243
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:29,380
But Hollerith was weary.

244
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:33,080
He was diagnosed with a bad heart in order to slow down.

245
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:36,300
In 1911, Hollerith sold his shares

246
00:17:36,301 --> 00:17:39,200
in the tabulating machine company for over $1 million.

247
00:17:39,201 --> 00:17:39,901
dollars.

248
00:17:39,902 --> 00:17:46,380
Hollerith's former company was merged with three others and led by master salesman

249
00:17:46,381 --> 00:17:52,856
Thomas Watson grew into a major supplier of business equipment.

250
00:17:52,857 --> 00:17:55,220
In 1924, Watson renamed

251
00:17:55,221 --> 00:17:59,425
the enterprise International Business Machines, IBM.

252
00:17:59,426 --> 00:18:02,660
Because of Hollerith, the name IBM would

253
00:18:02,661 --> 00:18:07,100
become synonymous with computers.

254
00:18:07,101 --> 00:18:10,980
By the 1930s, as America limped out of the Great Depression,

255
00:18:10,981 --> 00:18:13,700
companies like Burroughs and IBM foresaw

256
00:18:13,701 --> 00:18:15,420
continued growth and success.

257
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:21,840
Over the next decade, progress would be slow.

258
00:18:21,841 --> 00:18:24,780
It would take the destructive forces of World War II

259
00:18:24,781 --> 00:18:27,900
to give the computer its next great advance.

260
00:18:32,660 --> 00:18:39,400
World War II spurred the development of the true computer.

261
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:43,440
And in the turbulent days before the German blitzkrieg smashed Poland,

262
00:18:43,441 --> 00:18:49,120
a young Polish engineer walked into the British Embassy in Warsaw and made an astounding proposition.

263
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:55,740
He offered to sell the British the secret to the unbreakable German code machine, the Enigma.

264
00:18:57,180 --> 00:19:00,800
The British desperately wanted to crack the Enigma machine,

265
00:19:00,801 --> 00:19:05,900
used by German commanders to encrypt their most secret military radio messages.

266
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:11,800
Radio is a wonderful piece of technology, but the very beauty of radio,

267
00:19:12,020 --> 00:19:17,520
the fact that you broadcast messages out, makes it very bad for military traffic

268
00:19:17,521 --> 00:19:22,560
because the enemy can read your messages as well as the person you intended to read.

269
00:19:22,740 --> 00:19:25,160
So you encrypt the message.

270
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:29,820
British intelligence supplied the engineer with a fake diplomatic passport

271
00:19:29,821 --> 00:19:31,820
and smuggled him out of Warsaw.

272
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:36,320
While guarded by French agents in Paris,

273
00:19:36,321 --> 00:19:40,820
the engineer provided details on the code machine's ingenious operation.

274
00:19:40,821 --> 00:19:46,820
In the Enigma, plugs were rearranged to conform to that day's codebook combination.

275
00:19:46,821 --> 00:19:54,820
The power of Enigma was that this plug arrangement constantly varied how letters were coded throughout a transmission.

276
00:19:54,821 --> 00:19:58,820
The number of letter variations was astronomical.

277
00:19:58,821 --> 00:20:05,920
So high, the Germans considered their code machine to be unbreakable.

278
00:20:05,921 --> 00:20:08,780
But the British now knew how the machine worked.

279
00:20:08,781 --> 00:20:12,940
They realized that they could very quickly try different key combinations on a small

280
00:20:12,941 --> 00:20:14,800
part of the code.

281
00:20:14,801 --> 00:20:22,840
Then when that small part was broken and the key revealed, the rest could be decoded effortlessly.

282
00:20:22,841 --> 00:20:27,520
North of London at a secret installation in Bletchley Park, British code breakers built

283
00:20:27,521 --> 00:20:31,214
a computer-like machine to do just that, Colossus.

284
00:20:31,215 --> 00:20:34,760
Colossus used over 2,000 vacuum tubes to process

285
00:20:34,761 --> 00:20:36,927
25,000 characters per second.

286
00:20:36,928 --> 00:20:41,860
Colossus could only do one job, but it could compute very quickly.

287
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:47,380
Its deciphered German transmissions were called the Ultra Secret, the most closely guarded secret

288
00:20:47,381 --> 00:20:48,096
of the war.

289
00:20:48,097 --> 00:20:53,300
While the British were now able to read German messages, the question was how to

290
00:20:53,301 --> 00:20:57,720
take advantage of the secrets without tipping off the Germans that their code had been compromised.

291
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:04,840
The most senior Allied commanders were privy to ultra-material, but had to exercise caution in

292
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:08,058
reacting to it so as not to tip their hand.

293
00:21:08,059 --> 00:21:11,500
Ultra-information was never revealed to anyone

294
00:21:11,501 --> 00:21:14,105
in a position to be captured by the enemy.

295
00:21:14,106 --> 00:21:17,020
Field commanders often went into battle lacking

296
00:21:17,021 --> 00:21:21,860
information on the enemy that was known to their superiors from ultra-dispatches.

297
00:21:21,861 --> 00:21:28,860
This secrecy may have also kept Colossus from prominence in the history of computers.

298
00:21:32,860 --> 00:21:38,860
While Colossus was breaking German codes, across the Atlantic another computational device was under construction.

299
00:21:38,861 --> 00:21:45,860
The machine, which would directly influence the design of all future computers, was being built in Philadelphia.

300
00:21:51,860 --> 00:22:05,320
In response to the attack on Pearl Harbor, American industry quickly became a powerhouse

301
00:22:05,321 --> 00:22:09,800
producer of the implements of war.

302
00:22:09,801 --> 00:22:15,840
But by 1943, there was a critical shortage of a surprising component of the war machine,

303
00:22:15,841 --> 00:22:20,440
firing tables for artillery pieces.

304
00:22:20,441 --> 00:22:25,800
Tables allowed gunners to correctly aim their guns in different ranges, altitudes, temperatures

305
00:22:25,801 --> 00:22:27,500
and wind conditions.

306
00:22:27,501 --> 00:22:33,480
To calculate these tables required enormous numbers of calculations which at that time

307
00:22:33,481 --> 00:22:36,040
were done by human beings.

308
00:22:36,041 --> 00:22:41,280
Incidentally these people who did them were called computers, that was their job title.

309
00:22:41,281 --> 00:22:46,520
And they operated adding machines, mechanical adding machines primarily.

310
00:22:46,521 --> 00:22:52,120
And they would simply step through these calculations and produce these tables.

311
00:22:52,121 --> 00:22:56,200
One of the centers of firing table calculation was at the Moore School of Engineering at

312
00:22:56,201 --> 00:22:59,740
the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia.

313
00:22:59,741 --> 00:23:06,140
Midway through the war, it became clear that the tables could not be produced fast enough.

314
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:07,820
This was a crisis.

315
00:23:07,821 --> 00:23:13,060
Without tables, new guns could not be shipped to the troops overseas.

316
00:23:13,061 --> 00:23:20,400
To break the bottleneck, a Moore School physicist, John Mockley, made a fantastic proposal.

317
00:23:20,401 --> 00:23:25,220
He suggested that he could build a giant electronic computer that would be able to figure a single

318
00:23:25,221 --> 00:23:29,060
trajectory in 100 seconds.

319
00:23:29,061 --> 00:23:33,860
The Army, desperate for a device to help them win the war, reluctantly committed to the

320
00:23:33,861 --> 00:23:38,300
proposed cost of half a million dollars.

321
00:23:38,301 --> 00:23:41,240
and a brilliant graduate student in electrical engineering,

322
00:23:41,620 --> 00:23:44,800
Presper Eckert, set to work constructing ENIAC,

323
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:47,760
the Electronic Numeric Integrator and Computer.

324
00:23:50,020 --> 00:23:52,820
Driven by the knowledge that friends and relatives

325
00:23:52,821 --> 00:23:55,520
were dying in battle while they worked in Philadelphia,

326
00:23:56,260 --> 00:23:59,060
the team of young engineers toiled incessantly.

327
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:02,340
But could they create such a monster?

328
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:07,160
Everything had to be invented from square one,

329
00:24:07,161 --> 00:24:10,280
and then they had to build it and then they had to test it

330
00:24:10,281 --> 00:24:13,460
and they had to put it all together and make it work reliably.

331
00:24:14,080 --> 00:24:15,700
And then they had to learn how to program it.

332
00:24:16,320 --> 00:24:19,060
Nothing close to ENIAC had ever been conceived.

333
00:24:19,840 --> 00:24:22,840
Nearly 100 feet long and weighing 30 tons,

334
00:24:22,841 --> 00:24:26,140
it contained almost 70,000 resistors,

335
00:24:26,560 --> 00:24:29,680
10,000 capacitors, 6,000 switches,

336
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:33,360
and 18,000 delicate vacuum tubes.

337
00:24:33,361 --> 00:24:37,360
Vacuum tubes burn out just like light bulbs.

338
00:24:37,361 --> 00:24:40,360
In a machine that contained 18,000 vacuum tubes,

339
00:24:40,361 --> 00:24:45,360
it was likely that at least one would always be burned out, crippling the machine.

340
00:24:45,361 --> 00:24:49,360
Presper Eckert found the key to making ENIAC function.

341
00:24:49,361 --> 00:24:52,360
He had the vacuum tubes built to high tolerances.

342
00:24:52,361 --> 00:24:56,360
He critically tested them, and then he ran them at low power.

343
00:24:56,361 --> 00:25:00,360
If you took these measures, you might be able to get the machine to work

344
00:25:00,361 --> 00:25:06,760
machine to work for 10 minutes half an hour at a time since the machine calculated so quickly you

345
00:25:06,761 --> 00:25:12,920
can get a lot of work done in a half hour and so that's what happened after two years of intense

346
00:25:12,921 --> 00:25:19,480
work eniac was complete a few months after the japanese surrender although it wasn't finished

347
00:25:19,481 --> 00:25:26,360
in time to help win the war eniac was a marvelous machine huge and hot it could perform up to 5 000

348
00:25:26,361 --> 00:25:34,900
additions, 357 multiplications, and 38 divisions every second.

349
00:25:34,901 --> 00:25:40,120
By far and away the most complex machine of its time, ENIAC still lacked many of the qualities

350
00:25:40,121 --> 00:25:41,960
of a modern computer.

351
00:25:41,961 --> 00:25:43,940
Its memory was very primitive.

352
00:25:43,941 --> 00:25:48,600
It had to be laboriously rewired each time it was programmed and couldn't make logical

353
00:25:48,601 --> 00:25:51,680
decisions based on its calculations.

354
00:25:51,681 --> 00:25:57,060
But with tremendous expenditures of time and money, ENIAC had proved that computers could

355
00:25:57,061 --> 00:25:58,560
be constructed.

356
00:25:58,561 --> 00:26:06,080
However, except for arcane scientific calculation, did anyone really want them?

357
00:26:06,081 --> 00:26:19,520
The question lingered, could anyone build a really practical computer?

358
00:26:19,521 --> 00:26:24,200
Just before the end of the Second World War, an advisor to the ENIAC project, John von

359
00:26:24,201 --> 00:26:30,900
Neumann, wrote a paper that was to greatly affect the next stage of computer design.

360
00:26:30,901 --> 00:26:36,100
Von Neumann possessed a photographic memory, an incomparably fast mind, and was one of

361
00:26:36,101 --> 00:26:41,640
the principal scientists involved in the Manhattan Project, the building of the atomic bomb.

362
00:26:41,641 --> 00:26:44,620
He was also an advisor on ENIAC.

363
00:26:44,621 --> 00:26:50,440
The paper von Neumann wrote after the war delineated the structure of a modern computer.

364
00:26:50,441 --> 00:26:55,720
The paper drew heavily on the work building the ENIAC, yet was undeniably augmented by

365
00:26:55,721 --> 00:26:58,020
von Neumann's brilliance.

366
00:26:58,021 --> 00:27:04,780
Von Neumann's computer was to have a processing unit, a controlling unit, memory, input and

367
00:27:04,781 --> 00:27:06,280
output.

368
00:27:06,281 --> 00:27:11,480
But most importantly in the evolution of computers, it would hold its programming internally in

369
00:27:11,481 --> 00:27:14,260
its memory.

370
00:27:14,261 --> 00:27:18,260
Internally held programs give computers their power and versatility,

371
00:27:18,261 --> 00:27:25,260
because an internal program can modify what it does based on data or the results of computations.

372
00:27:25,261 --> 00:27:30,260
In machines like the ENIAC, programming had been hard-wired or fixed,

373
00:27:30,261 --> 00:27:33,260
so the machine was much less adaptable.

374
00:27:33,261 --> 00:27:39,260
The idea for storing the program internally was the last key to developing the true computer.

375
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:43,780
But whether it was von Neumann's idea has long been hotly debated.

376
00:27:44,660 --> 00:27:47,840
Eckert and Mockley claimed they formulated internal programs

377
00:27:47,841 --> 00:27:50,540
as a natural part of their work building ENIAC,

378
00:27:50,900 --> 00:27:54,140
although they couldn't stop and incorporate the idea into the machine.

379
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:58,320
But many who read the paper assumed that all the genius behind it

380
00:27:58,321 --> 00:28:00,140
was from the great von Neumann.

381
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:05,200
He was one of the most widely regarded mathematicians in the world.

382
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:08,440
Eckert and Mockley were relatively obscure.

383
00:28:08,441 --> 00:28:12,380
Eckert was a young man just out of school.

384
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:16,560
Mockley had been a professor at a fairly out-of-the-way college.

385
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,740
They didn't have international reputations.

386
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:24,040
Eckert and Mockley weren't included as authors in the paper.

387
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:25,880
They felt they had been betrayed.

388
00:28:27,100 --> 00:28:31,320
The most important effect of von Neumann's paper was to spur computer development.

389
00:28:32,120 --> 00:28:34,720
Eckert and Mockley moved into offices in Philadelphia,

390
00:28:34,721 --> 00:28:38,660
hired a staff and set up a company that would build a business computer.

391
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:40,880
They called it the UNIVAC.

392
00:28:42,320 --> 00:28:46,740
They signed a fixed-cost contract to build a UNIVAC for the Census Bureau,

393
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:49,320
rolled up their sleeves, and went to work.

394
00:28:51,260 --> 00:28:55,260
Unfortunately, building UNIVAC turned out to be a monumental undertaking.

395
00:28:56,040 --> 00:29:00,400
As they struggled to make UNIVAC real, they also struggled financially

396
00:29:00,401 --> 00:29:03,840
and needed to be bailed out by a series of larger companies.

397
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:09,840
They eventually joined forces with Remington Rand, a flourishing typewriter manufacturer.

398
00:29:12,160 --> 00:29:19,120
In March of 1951, after six years of toil, they finally delivered the first UNIVAC to the Census

399
00:29:19,121 --> 00:29:19,821
Bureau.

400
00:29:19,822 --> 00:29:27,760
Unlike ENIAC, the UNIVAC was an entire computer system designed for business. UNIVAC

401
00:29:27,761 --> 00:29:33,380
could be programmed for a variety of data processing tasks. Compact tape

402
00:29:33,381 --> 00:29:38,560
drives held data and results were automatically printed. But even with the

403
00:29:38,561 --> 00:29:43,960
backing of Remington Rand, sales remained slow. Very few people understood how

404
00:29:43,961 --> 00:29:51,340
useful a computer could be. That perception changed dramatically one night

405
00:29:51,341 --> 00:29:53,340
in 1952.

406
00:29:57,340 --> 00:29:59,340
In a brilliant public relations move,

407
00:29:59,341 --> 00:30:01,340
Remington Rand arranged with CBS

408
00:30:01,341 --> 00:30:03,340
to use a UNIVAC on election night

409
00:30:03,341 --> 00:30:05,340
to predict the outcome of the presidential race

410
00:30:05,341 --> 00:30:09,340
between Dwight Eisenhower and Adlai Stevenson.

411
00:30:09,341 --> 00:30:12,340
Polls said the race was too close to call.

412
00:30:14,340 --> 00:30:16,340
No one had ever programmed a computer

413
00:30:16,341 --> 00:30:18,340
to make electoral predictions.

414
00:30:18,341 --> 00:30:20,340
Eckerd and Mockley's engineers

415
00:30:20,341 --> 00:30:23,340
entered their customized algorithms right until airtime.

416
00:30:26,340 --> 00:30:29,340
The operators fed in the results of selected eastern precincts

417
00:30:29,341 --> 00:30:32,340
and at 9 p.m. ran their program.

418
00:30:32,341 --> 00:30:35,340
UNIVAC predicted a landslide for Eisenhower,

419
00:30:35,341 --> 00:30:38,340
but the polls said differently.

420
00:30:38,341 --> 00:30:41,340
The operators didn't believe what UNIVAC was telling them

421
00:30:41,341 --> 00:30:44,340
and assumed their programming was at fault.

422
00:30:44,341 --> 00:30:46,340
Quickly, they reprogrammed the machine

423
00:30:46,341 --> 00:30:48,340
to better reflect what the experts predicted.

424
00:30:48,341 --> 00:30:54,360
As the night went on, it became clear that Eisenhower would indeed win by a landslide.

425
00:30:55,660 --> 00:30:59,220
CBS sheepishly announced that they hadn't believed the machines.

426
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:06,740
When all the votes were tallied, Univac's initial prediction was off by less than 1% of the final result,

427
00:31:07,040 --> 00:31:09,780
an extraordinary prediction even by today's standards.

428
00:31:11,700 --> 00:31:15,540
The power and utility of the computer had been proved.

429
00:31:18,340 --> 00:31:29,340
After the success of UNIVAC, various companies began to see a future in computer development.

430
00:31:29,341 --> 00:31:33,840
New companies like Burroughs, as well as old giants like General Electric, jumped into

431
00:31:33,841 --> 00:31:37,440
the computer business.

432
00:31:37,441 --> 00:31:42,260
But most large American businesses were dependent on the data processing systems provided by

433
00:31:42,261 --> 00:31:50,500
one company, the office machine monolith IBM.

434
00:31:50,501 --> 00:31:54,860
IBM had no computers.

435
00:31:54,861 --> 00:32:01,020
IBM's aging leader, the legendary sales guru Thomas Watson Sr., was not eager to jump into

436
00:32:01,021 --> 00:32:04,440
the enormously costly development of computers.

437
00:32:04,441 --> 00:32:11,620
A computer back then contained thousands of vacuum tubes, occupied one or more large

438
00:32:11,621 --> 00:32:17,500
rooms and required a small army of attendants to run.

439
00:32:17,501 --> 00:32:23,760
IBM and most other people didn't see how computers could be used in business.

440
00:32:23,761 --> 00:32:29,760
It took Watson's son, Tom Watson, Jr., to lead IBM into the computer age.

441
00:32:29,761 --> 00:32:35,380
As Watson himself recalls, the move was spurred by IBM's customers who were fed up with bulky

442
00:32:35,381 --> 00:32:36,380
punch cards.

443
00:32:36,381 --> 00:32:42,700
I remember particularly Jim Madden, then vice president of the Metropolitan Life, who said,

444
00:32:42,820 --> 00:32:46,960
we're going to cancel our IBM just the minute we learned to do this in tapes,

445
00:32:47,060 --> 00:32:53,640
because three floors of the Metropolitan Life building are used to store the cards of our customers' accounts.

446
00:32:53,820 --> 00:32:57,720
And if we keep going the way we will, they'll occupy the whole building.

447
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:00,460
We were threatened into this progress.

448
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:03,920
Faced with the prospect of losing customers,

449
00:33:03,921 --> 00:33:07,080
Tom Watson, Jr. ordered the development of a computer.

450
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:13,020
IBM's famous sales force told their customers a computer would arrive soon.

451
00:33:13,640 --> 00:33:15,500
In fact, it was still being planned.

452
00:33:16,520 --> 00:33:20,820
Finally, in 1953, IBM unveiled the 701.

453
00:33:22,020 --> 00:33:24,740
Although technologically inferior to the UNIVAC,

454
00:33:25,060 --> 00:33:29,200
the 701 and other early IBM computers were hits with the customers

455
00:33:29,201 --> 00:33:33,040
because they conformed to standard IBM systems and support.

456
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:37,920
IBM saw the future, and it was computers.

457
00:33:37,921 --> 00:33:41,920
They redirected corporate efforts to computer development.

458
00:33:41,921 --> 00:33:45,920
IBM's efforts paid off, and by the beginning of the 1960s,

459
00:33:45,921 --> 00:33:49,920
the company's large mainframe computers dominated the business.

460
00:33:49,921 --> 00:33:55,920
But the development of the computer was about to go in an altogether different direction.

461
00:34:03,920 --> 00:34:17,920
We choose to go to the moon in this decade and do the other things, not because they

462
00:34:18,320 --> 00:34:21,600
are easy, but because they are hard.

463
00:34:21,601 --> 00:34:27,880
In 1961, America was trailing Russia in the race for space.

464
00:34:27,881 --> 00:34:37,400
1961, a year of achievement for Soviet scientists in the race for space.

465
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:44,380
Yuri Gagarin has become the first human to orbit the Earth,

466
00:34:45,020 --> 00:34:49,320
and crowds in Moscow's Red Square salute the 27-year-old cosmonaut.

467
00:34:50,120 --> 00:34:52,920
As NASA engineers began planning the lunar mission,

468
00:34:52,921 --> 00:34:57,440
they realized a computer as powerful as one currently the size of a room

469
00:34:57,441 --> 00:35:04,020
must be on board. The engineers wondered, is such a small computer even possible?

470
00:35:04,940 --> 00:35:09,900
The first great breakthrough that would lead to computer miniaturization had already been made

471
00:35:09,901 --> 00:35:17,700
on December 23, 1947, when three scientists at Bell Labs, William Shockley, Walter Breitain,

472
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:23,360
and John Bardeen, invented the transistor. Formed on the semiconductor silicone,

473
00:35:23,361 --> 00:35:28,360
the transistor could replace large vacuum tubes in computers.

474
00:35:28,361 --> 00:35:34,360
Compared to vacuum tubes, transistors were tiny, required little power, and produced little heat.

475
00:35:34,361 --> 00:35:42,360
The breakthrough was sufficiently important that the three inventors of the transistor were awarded the Nobel Prize.

476
00:35:42,361 --> 00:35:47,360
A computer that could navigate to the moon and back would require thousands of transistors,

477
00:35:47,361 --> 00:35:51,360
and although small, they were not nearly small enough.

478
00:35:51,361 --> 00:36:02,521
The next step in miniaturization occurred in 1959, when Robert Noyce and Jack Kilby, engineers for rival transistor manufacturers, independently came up with

479
00:36:02,522 --> 00:36:06,360
breakthroughs that led to the same revolutionary idea.

480
00:36:06,361 --> 00:36:16,720
An entire network of electronic components, transistors, diodes, capacitors, and resistors could be incorporated onto a single chip of silicon.

481
00:36:16,721 --> 00:36:23,720
This great innovation in electronics was called the integrated circuit.

482
00:36:23,721 --> 00:36:31,720
Using integrated circuits, a 10-ounce computer was built that was as powerful as a 30-pound one made of transistors.

483
00:36:31,721 --> 00:36:35,720
But integrated circuits had an inherent problem.

484
00:36:35,721 --> 00:36:39,720
They were difficult to manufacture and therefore were expensive.

485
00:36:39,721 --> 00:36:44,720
But the space race had started just in time to pay for them.

486
00:36:44,721 --> 00:36:52,900
In 1969, 5,000 integrated circuits made up the heart of each of two identical computers,

487
00:36:52,901 --> 00:36:56,620
one on the lunar orbiter and one on the lander.

488
00:36:56,621 --> 00:37:03,100
For their size, these were the most powerful computers on Earth, soon to leave the Earth

489
00:37:03,101 --> 00:37:04,100
entirely.

490
00:37:04,101 --> 00:37:07,100
Beautiful, just beautiful.

491
00:37:07,101 --> 00:37:14,120
I'm going to step off the land now.

492
00:37:14,121 --> 00:37:17,720
As Neil Armstrong took one small step in the lunar dust,

493
00:37:17,721 --> 00:37:23,020
Intel engineer Ted Hoff was making the last great leap in miniaturization,

494
00:37:23,021 --> 00:37:27,920
developing an idea that would put an entire computer on a chip of silicone,

495
00:37:27,921 --> 00:37:30,320
the microprocessor.

496
00:37:30,321 --> 00:37:34,820
The genie would be out of its bottle, beginning the computer revolution,

497
00:37:34,821 --> 00:37:37,820
and changing the world forever.

498
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:58,120
Apollo 13, intended as the third lunar landing, had just lost two fuel cells and was venting

499
00:37:58,820 --> 00:38:04,680
oxygen into space, 200,000 miles from Earth.

500
00:38:04,681 --> 00:38:08,480
Soon the second oxygen tank would begin losing pressure.

501
00:38:08,481 --> 00:38:13,300
The astronauts would die unless they could precisely align their spacecraft and fire

502
00:38:13,301 --> 00:38:18,700
their rocket to slingshot themselves around the moon and back to Earth.

503
00:38:18,701 --> 00:38:23,300
Critical to the alignment was Apollo's state-of-the-art guidance computer.

504
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:25,120
A new trajectory was figured.

505
00:38:25,121 --> 00:38:27,960
The all-or-nothing rocket firing was made.

506
00:38:27,961 --> 00:38:33,620
The Apollo 13 crew miraculously returned to Earth with the help of their small and powerful

507
00:38:33,621 --> 00:38:35,280
computer.

508
00:38:35,281 --> 00:38:39,720
By the last Apollo mission two and one-half years later, computers as powerful as those

509
00:38:39,721 --> 00:38:43,020
on Apollo would be available to everyone.

510
00:38:43,021 --> 00:38:48,480
That was because soon before the Apollo 13 flight, an engineer at Intel, Ted Hoff, had

511
00:38:48,481 --> 00:38:51,180
come up with an ingenious idea.

512
00:38:51,181 --> 00:38:56,720
Hoff had been told to design 12 separate integrated circuits to make a Japanese pocket calculator.

513
00:38:56,721 --> 00:39:01,700
He suggested placing the entire processing unit on a chip and programming it just like

514
00:39:01,701 --> 00:39:03,580
a computer.

515
00:39:03,581 --> 00:39:10,900
Intel developed the idea and by 1970 they had a working model of a microprocessor.

516
00:39:10,901 --> 00:39:17,400
It was the invention, not just of integrated circuits, but of a particular kind of integrated

517
00:39:17,401 --> 00:39:22,900
circuit, the microprocessor that makes today's personal computers possible.

518
00:39:22,901 --> 00:39:28,020
Smaller than a fingernail, a microprocessor contains many of the components of a computer,

519
00:39:28,021 --> 00:39:35,580
including a control unit, a clock, and areas where data can be stored and modified.

520
00:39:35,581 --> 00:39:44,060
power was about to become very cheap and very compact in the mid-1970s two friends

521
00:39:44,061 --> 00:39:49,820
stephen wozniak and steve jobs were manufacturing a small computer in a palo alto garage

522
00:39:51,340 --> 00:39:57,580
steve jobs was a college dropout but he was a college dropout with a difference he was very

523
00:39:57,581 --> 00:40:04,220
intelligent he had a lot of street smarts he was also extremely ambitious i think perhaps more

524
00:40:04,221 --> 00:40:05,709
important than that.

525
00:40:05,710 --> 00:40:11,220
He knew the other Steve, Steve Wozniak, had created something exceptional.

526
00:40:11,221 --> 00:40:17,480
Steve Jobs sold his Volkswagen and Steven Wozniak sold his HP calculator to finance

527
00:40:17,481 --> 00:40:21,353
their company that would revolutionize the computer industry.

528
00:40:21,354 --> 00:40:22,940
Jobs trekked all over the

529
00:40:22,941 --> 00:40:28,440
San Francisco Bay Area to find buyers for the $500 machine, which they called the Apple

530
00:40:28,441 --> 00:40:31,940
One. The Apple One was large and unwieldy.

531
00:40:31,941 --> 00:40:35,440
Jobs realized they needed a new design for

532
00:40:35,960 --> 00:40:41,608
a computer that anyone could use. Wozniak began to build the Apple Two.

533
00:40:41,609 --> 00:40:42,960
The fate of Apple

534
00:40:42,961 --> 00:40:49,560
changed dramatically when in the fall of 1976, a visitor to Wozniak's garage saw the prototype

535
00:40:49,561 --> 00:40:50,787
of the Apple Two.

536
00:40:50,788 --> 00:40:56,560
The visitor was Mike Markola, who at 32 had retired from Intel as a millionaire.

537
00:40:56,561 --> 00:41:02,020
He was so impressed by the Apple II that he joined Apple and put it on a sound business footing.

538
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:06,560
The Apple II was introduced to the public and sales skyrocketed.

539
00:41:06,900 --> 00:41:12,840
But in 1978, even with the success of the Apple II, using a computer wasn't easy.

540
00:41:13,480 --> 00:41:22,400
The early Apple computers, as well as all early personal computers, did not have graphical interfaces.

541
00:41:22,401 --> 00:41:24,100
They didn't have mice.

542
00:41:26,020 --> 00:41:30,420
They had what's known as a command line interface.

543
00:41:30,860 --> 00:41:33,540
That is, you typed instructions into the computer,

544
00:41:33,980 --> 00:41:37,200
and your instructions appeared as text on the screen.

545
00:41:39,780 --> 00:41:45,680
A simple-to-use computer had been conceived by a computer scientist named Doug Engelbart.

546
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:51,540
He demonstrated his vision in 1968 at the Fall Joint Computer Conference

547
00:41:51,541 --> 00:41:52,540
in San Francisco.

548
00:41:52,541 --> 00:41:56,540
If I hit W, it'll say delete word.

549
00:41:56,541 --> 00:42:00,540
The arrow moves back and forth to give me feedback.

550
00:42:00,541 --> 00:42:05,040
Wielding a keyboard and a pointing device he called a mouse, Engelbart worked with a

551
00:42:05,041 --> 00:42:13,260
computer 30 miles away, linked by microwaves, and demonstrated word processing and hypertext.

552
00:42:13,261 --> 00:42:18,540
Many in the audience went home inspired, but one group alone was to fulfill Engelbart's

553
00:42:18,541 --> 00:42:20,380
vision.

554
00:42:20,381 --> 00:42:26,040
That group was just down the road from San Francisco at Xerox Park.

555
00:42:26,041 --> 00:42:32,980
In 1970, Xerox dominated the copier industry, but thought the future might be in computers.

556
00:42:32,981 --> 00:42:38,380
A young Xerox executive, Robert Taylor, worked with a team to transform the way the computer

557
00:42:38,381 --> 00:42:40,340
industry was perceived.

558
00:42:40,341 --> 00:42:42,500
As Robert Taylor himself remembers...

559
00:42:42,501 --> 00:42:49,880
The chairman of Xerox at the time, Peter McCullough, made a speech where he said that

560
00:42:49,881 --> 00:42:53,933
Xerox was going to become the architecture of information.

561
00:42:53,934 --> 00:42:55,820
So I asked his speechwriter

562
00:42:55,821 --> 00:42:58,966
not too long after that, what did that mean?

563
00:42:58,967 --> 00:43:02,540
Because I had an idea about what it ought to mean,

564
00:43:02,541 --> 00:43:04,156
and so did some other people.

565
00:43:04,157 --> 00:43:07,220
And the speechwriter said, well, he didn't really know,

566
00:43:07,221 --> 00:43:12,200
but it's a ringing phrase. And so I said, well, we're gonna make it happen.

567
00:43:12,201 --> 00:43:18,500
Robert Taylor hired many of the country's top computer scientists and challenged them to create

568
00:43:18,501 --> 00:43:22,500
an easy-to-use personal computer.

569
00:43:22,501 --> 00:43:28,500
The result was the Alto, which incorporated many of the innovations in personal computers we take for granted today,

570
00:43:28,501 --> 00:43:32,500
all developed at Xerox PARC.

571
00:43:32,501 --> 00:43:38,500
The Alto used a mouse, a graphical interface, built-in networking, and printed on a laser printer.

572
00:43:38,501 --> 00:43:45,500
Xerox developed the STAR, the commercial model of the Alto, but it never sold well.

573
00:43:45,501 --> 00:43:52,020
Xerox was a large and very successful photocopier company,

574
00:43:52,021 --> 00:43:55,700
and it didn't really understand computers.

575
00:43:56,260 --> 00:44:01,080
It didn't appreciate the brilliance, the originality,

576
00:44:01,540 --> 00:44:05,060
and the enormous commercial worth

577
00:44:05,061 --> 00:44:09,980
of the computer developments at Xerox PARC.

578
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:12,100
But Steve Jobs did.

579
00:44:15,500 --> 00:44:24,780
In 1979, Steve Jobs visited Xerox PARC and saw the Alto.

580
00:44:24,781 --> 00:44:30,500
He returned to Apple and immediately set to work on what would become the Macintosh computer,

581
00:44:30,980 --> 00:44:34,600
the first popular personal computer similar to those used today.

582
00:44:35,380 --> 00:44:39,660
What made the Macintosh easy to use was its operating system and applications,

583
00:44:39,940 --> 00:44:41,540
otherwise known as software.

584
00:44:41,541 --> 00:44:46,540
Increasingly, software was dominating the advances made in computers.

585
00:44:46,541 --> 00:44:55,540
Bill Gates, the young president of computing software company Microsoft, understood the importance of software to the future of computers,

586
00:44:55,541 --> 00:45:01,540
and parlayed this vision into a vast software empire, making him the richest man in the world.

587
00:45:01,541 --> 00:45:06,540
The dream of a machine that could think had come from a mechanical device to an electronic one,

588
00:45:06,541 --> 00:45:13,580
where the bits of coded information that ran it were as important, perhaps more important, than the machine that they controlled.

589
00:45:14,660 --> 00:45:20,820
This may have been the best evidence that a thinking machine had arrived and could now be placed on your desk.

590
00:45:22,280 --> 00:45:25,940
Soon, nearly half the jobs in America would use the computer.

591
00:45:26,560 --> 00:45:34,700
Within the decade, microprocessors would be everywhere, incorporated into automobiles, appliances, and scientific instruments,

592
00:45:34,701 --> 00:45:39,700
significantly increasing their capability and reliability.

593
00:45:41,700 --> 00:45:46,700
Unprecedented fortunes would be made in businesses that hadn't existed a decade before.

594
00:45:46,701 --> 00:45:55,700
Education would be transformed forever as the access to information would be transferred from libraries and universities to your desktop.

595
00:45:55,701 --> 00:46:05,060
The Earth would shrink with unprecedented speed as a worldwide communication grid became accessible to anyone with a computer and modem.

596
00:46:07,040 --> 00:46:11,360
And even now, the evolution of a thinking machine isn't finished.

597
00:46:12,100 --> 00:46:20,640
Computers have already changed the way we live, and they'll change the way we explore our world and other worlds in the 21st century.

598
00:46:20,641 --> 00:46:27,640
They will take us to distant galaxies and they will connect us right here on Earth.

599
00:46:27,641 --> 00:46:31,640
Computers will continue to become more interconnected.

600
00:46:31,641 --> 00:46:35,640
They will continue to become smaller, faster, cheaper.

601
00:46:35,641 --> 00:46:39,640
And software will become more powerful.

602
00:46:39,641 --> 00:46:47,640
Minds that have yet to be formed will mold this power to create new marvels inconceivable to us today.

